WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.888
Security levels express increasing degrees of protection for industrial automation and control functions.

00:00:04.888 --> 00:00:08.939
They provide a structured way to translate risk into security expectations.

00:00:08.939 --> 00:00:13.727
A higher level represents resistance to a more capable, motivated, and well-resourced threat,

00:00:13.727 --> 00:00:17.042
but it also requires stronger capabilities, implementation, and evidence.

00:00:17.042 --> 00:00:22.935
The target security level describes the protection required for a zone or conduit based on risk.

00:00:22.935 --> 00:00:26.986
It should result from analysis of threats, consequences, exposure, existing safeguards,

00:00:26.986 --> 00:00:28.091
and risk tolerance.

00:00:28.091 --> 00:00:32.511
It is not selected simply because a system is important or because

00:00:32.511 --> 00:00:34.720
a supplier markets a high level.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:39.140
A component's capability security level describes the security capability it can support

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:40.982
when correctly configured and used.

00:00:40.982 --> 00:00:45.769
The achieved security level describes what the integrated and operated system actually provides.

00:00:45.769 --> 00:00:48.348
These concepts are related but not interchangeable.

00:00:48.348 --> 00:00:52.399
A capable device can be undermined by poor architecture, shared credentials,

00:00:52.399 --> 00:00:54.609
weak remote access, or disabled features.

00:00:54.609 --> 00:00:59.765
Security level selection should be specific to the foundational requirements and the system context.

00:00:59.765 --> 00:01:05.658
A zone may need particularly strong integrity and restricted data flow while having different confidentiality needs.

00:01:05.658 --> 00:01:07.499
Independent safety functions, physical controls,

00:01:07.499 --> 00:01:09.709
and operational procedures also influence risk,

00:01:09.709 --> 00:01:12.655
but they should be documented rather than assumed.

00:01:12.655 --> 00:01:13.392
Verification matters.

00:01:13.392 --> 00:01:18.180
Teams should be able to trace a target level to risk decisions, requirements,

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:20.758
design controls, component capabilities, configuration, test results,

00:01:20.758 --> 00:01:21.863
and operational procedures.

00:01:21.863 --> 00:01:24.441
Changes to connectivity, process consequence, threat conditions,

00:01:24.441 --> 00:01:26.283
or components may require reassessment.

00:01:26.283 --> 00:01:29.229
Security levels are therefore decision and engineering tools.

00:01:29.229 --> 00:01:32.912
They do not certify an entire facility with one number,

00:01:32.912 --> 00:01:37.700
and they do not guarantee that a system remains secure throughout its life.
